<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/03/2012-03-04-saw-6.gif" alt="http://etc.usf.edu/clipart/42700/42768/cubes-6_42768.htm" width="173" height="200" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-25900" /></p>
<p>With best play on both sides, conventional tic-tac-toe (noughts and crosses) is always a draw. Played in three dimensions, though, in a 3&#215;3&#215;3 cube, it&#8217;s always a win for whichever player manages to take the central cell. In fact, a draw is impossible &#8212; there&#8217;s no way to fill the &#8220;board&#8221; with 14 crosses and 13 noughts without completing one or more winning paths.</p>
<p>What if we reverse the object so that the first player to complete such a path <em>loses</em>? Interestingly, in that case seizing the center cell is still the best strategy. Thereafter, whichever cell the second player chooses, the first player should take the cell diametrically opposite. Obviously this can&#8217;t create a winning triple that passes through the center, but neither can it create a triple that <em>doesn&#8217;t</em> pass through the center, because the second player would necessarily just have completed such a triple himself on the opposite side of the cube.</p>
<p>&#8220;So the first player cannot lose,&#8221; writes mathematician Solomon Golomb. &#8220;Thus, because no draws are possible on the 3&#215;3&#215;3 &#8216;board,&#8217; he must win!&#8221;</p>
<p>(This is similar to the first player&#8217;s strategy in <a href="https://www.futilitycloset.com/2010/08/13/the-cigar-puzzle/">Henry Dudeney&#8217;s cigar puzzle</a>.)</p>
<p>(Solomon W. Golomb, <a href="https://archive.org/details/ALifetimeOfPuzzles/page/n285/mode/2up">&#8220;Martin Gardner and Ticktacktoe,&#8221;</a> in Erik D. Demaine, Martin L. Demaine, and Tom Rodgers, eds., <em>A Lifetime of Puzzles</em>, 2008.)</p>
